YAAP Jermoe Bar-Mal-Hum-Neu

Jermoe the Barbarian was always a big lad. None too bright, mind, in
fact so thick he even spelt his name wrong (he was meant to be
Jerome.)

When he entered the dungeons he was Strengf 18, Dextetiry 18,
Constityooshun 18 with Inteligunce 7 and Wisdumb 8. Still, with his
mighty two-handed sword he met nothing in the dungeon or mines to
present him with a serious problem before he got to minetown (already
at XL9) except basic maths, reading and putting the underwear on
before the trousers.

(For wombats I always go mines first for the rapid XP gain and
plentiful food.)

The minetown priest was co-aligned (the first time in about 50 games),
and so the sacrificing began.

First he got cleaver, but as 2h sword user this was no use. Next he
got Giantslayer, which fitted with the longterm strategy of getting a
good long sword artifact, so he started twoweaponing long sword and
Giantslayer. Unfortunately he accidentally killed a watchman while
hallucinating so pulled out of mines for a bit.

Back in the dungeons the next significant events were finding a Ring
of slow digestion, and a Helm of Opposite Alignment. YIKES!
Autocursing is a bummer. This was the only magic helmet he would find
before the Astral Plane.

A nice big general store helped ID several scrolls, rings and potions.

Another co-aligned temple showed up which became his new base: lots of
organisation and sacrificing for weapons he didn't want like
Trollsbane and Mjollnir, but finally he got Vorpal Blade. All monies
were hoarded and traded for protection, getting to naked AC 0
eventually. Ludios was not in this game.

Sokoban was a major headache for one so dim, but in the end he got to
a BoH.

He started the quest but stayed off the last level, as a latent memory
of other over-confident barbarian deaths at the hands of 8 Trolls and
Ogres warned him of under-preparation. ("I'll be back" he muttered,
picking up another latent barbarian memory.)

Medusa and the castle were not far below. The back door route was as
always much less hazardous than trying to open the drawbridge. He
picked up a couple of useful bits and pieces in the back rooms before
opening the door behind the throne and shouting "Heeeeere's Johnny!"
He emptied half a dozen wands of lightning and fire into the crowd,
and took on what ever was left one at a time at the doorway.

Once this was all over he found the wand and wished for 2 scrols of
charging (got 1), GDSM, Speed boots, and GDSM again having blown up
the first one with overcharging (5 for armour, 7 for weapons; 5 for
armour, 7 for weapons...)

Now with MR he went back to finish the quest, with no difficulty.

Before going below the castle and with no wishes left, there was a
definite problem with high AC and missing AK items. So, armed with his
wand of polymorph, he made a HUGE pile of armour, wands, rings,
scrolls and potions. And I mean huge: it ran to 18 pages. Golems were
inevitable, but at the end he had a load of really useful stuff,
including a blessed +2 Cloak of Displacement (still the best cloak if
you already have MR IMHO), a shield of reflection and a +3 Hawaiian
shirt.

Looking good, Jermoe now had AC -39, XL 16, HP 210, Teleportitis with
a Ring Of TC, also a Ring of +3 increase damage, slow digestion and
conflict. The only down side was that the best sword he had found was
Vorpal Blade, but having been crowned it would take ages to get
anything else. So, no point hanging around: lets do hell.

>From here on it was plain sailing. Frost Brand turned up on the floor!

He couldn't find Vlad's up stairs for ages: burned out two crystal
balls and a stack of magic maps trying to find it. It was in Orcus
town in the end (pretending to be a mimic!)

Really smooth other than that. One trip back out for polypiling,
alchemy, identifying and sorting, then straight in for the book, the
square, the sanctum, the running, the mysterious force, the Wizard
("So, you thought.." Yeah, whatever, *Zap*) right up to Level 1.

Actually this was the first time I remembered to take a wand of secret
door detection to the sanctum: really helps.

Unusually, only one shopkeeper died in this game, and this was the one
who charged $2000 to get back Giantslayer when Jermoe dropped it after
eating French fried something or other in a general store. He paid,
but vowed vengance, which came on the way back to the surface at the
point of Frost Brand.

Again, the planes were very smooth due to a fully loaded crystal ball.
And I remembered the routine: When leaving Earth, get out crystal
ball, drop wands of digging and start levitating before going into the
portal. When leaving Water, dump crystal ball, remove ring of
conflict, put on ring of Free action and blindfold. Put back ring of
conflict as soon as the useless Angel is out of sight.

Famine was on the right, so he went that way. On entering the room our
hero was surrounded by A's on 7 sides, so broke a 0:1 wand of
teleportation and cleared them out. Very satisfying! The room was dark
but there didn't seem to be any obstacles, so he sprinted for the
door, got through and locked it without even seeing the big &.

The temple was Crom's, so after locking the door he picked up
everything point-scoring left by the player-monsters and offered the
amulet.


 


